<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-1-3-jvm" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Java Virtual Machine</title>

  <p>
    Before Java was created in the mid-1990s, programming languages generally fell into
    one of two distinct camps: they were either compiled or interpreted. Understanding
    how these two approaches work—and their respective drawbacks—makes it clear why
    Java’s architecture was so revolutionary.
  </p>

  <p>
    In traditionally compiled languages like C, C++, and Rust, a compiler takes the
    programmer's human-readable source code and translates the entire program directly
    into native machine language before the program is ever run:
  </p>

  <cd>
    Source Code (.cpp)  ----[ Compiler ]----&gt;  Native Machine Code (.exe)  ----&gt;  Runs directly on CPU
  </cd>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Advantage (Speed): Because the resulting executable file consists strictly of
        the CPU's native binary instructions, the computer executes it at maximum hardware
        speed with zero translation overhead.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Drawback (Platform Dependence): The generated machine code is permanently
        locked to one specific type of CPU architecture (such as Intel/AMD x86-64 or Apple
        Silicon ARM64) and a specific operating system (Windows, macOS, or Linux). An
        <c>.exe</c> file compiled on an Intel Windows PC is completely unintelligible to an
        ARM-based Mac or a Linux server.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Historically, software companies had to maintain separate codebases and recompile
    their software independently for every single hardware and operating system
    combination they wanted to support.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the opposite end of the spectrum are interpreted languages (such as Python,
    Ruby, and JavaScript).
  </p>

  <p>
    In a purely interpreted system, there is no separate ahead-of-time compilation step
    that creates a standalone binary file. Instead, another program called an interpreter
    reads the source code and carries out the instructions on the fly:
  </p>

  <cd>
    Source Code (.py)  ----&gt;  [ Interpreter Program ]  ----&gt;  Translates and executes line by line
  </cd>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Advantage (Portability): Because source files remain plain text, they are
        completely portable. You can write a Python script on Windows and run the exact same
        file on a Mac or a Linux server, provided that machine has a Python interpreter
        installed.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The Drawback (Execution Overhead): Translating code into machine instructions line
        by line while the program is actively running takes significant time.
        Historically, interpreted programs ran anywhere from 10 to 50 times slower than
        natively compiled C++ programs.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In 1995, James Gosling and his team at Sun Microsystems engineered a brilliant
    hybrid approach that combines the speed of compilation with the universal
    portability of interpretation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead of compiling directly to a physical chip's machine language, the Java
    compiler (<c>javac</c>) translates human-readable <c>.java</c> source code into an
    intermediate format called Java bytecode, which is saved in a <c>.class</c> file:
  </p>

  <cd>
    Source Code (.java)  ----[ javac Compiler ]----&gt;  Java Bytecode (.class)
  </cd>

  <p>
    Bytecode looks very much like machine language—it consists of primitive,
    binary-encoded instructions—but it is not designed for an Intel, AMD, or ARM
    processor. Instead, it is the machine language of an idealized, theoretical
    computer: the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-java-bytecode-instructions">
    <title>Example Java Bytecode Instructions</title>
    <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col width="28%"/>
      <col width="18%"/>
      <col width="16%"/>
      <col width="38%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Bytecode (Hex and Binary)</strong></cell>
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Bytecode Mnemonic</strong></cell>
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Operation</strong></cell>
        <cell halign="center"><strong>Description</strong></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <p><c>15 01</c></p>
          <p><c>(00010101 00000001)</c></p>
        </cell>
        <cell><c>iload 1</c></cell>
        <cell>Load</cell>
        <cell>
          Push the integer stored in local variable 1 onto the JVM operand stack.
          (Equivalent to LOAD R1)
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <p><c>15 02</c></p>
          <p><c>(00010101 00000010)</c></p>
        </cell>
        <cell><c>iload 2</c></cell>
        <cell>Load</cell>
        <cell>
          Push the integer stored in local variable 2 onto the JVM operand stack.
          (Equivalent to LOAD R2)
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <p><c>60</c></p>
          <p><c>(01100000)</c></p>
        </cell>
        <cell><c>iadd</c></cell>
        <cell>Arithmetic</cell>
        <cell>
          Pop the top two integers from the stack, add them together, and push the sum back
          onto the stack. (Equivalent to ADD)
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <p><c>36 03</c></p>
          <p><c>(00110110 00000011)</c></p>
        </cell>
        <cell><c>istore 3</c></cell>
        <cell>Store</cell>
        <cell>
          Pop the integer from the top of the stack and store it in local variable 3.
          (Equivalent to STORE R3)
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <p><c>a7 00 14</c></p>
          <p><c>(10100111 00000000 00010100)</c></p>
        </cell>
        <cell><c>goto 20</c></cell>
        <cell>Branch</cell>
        <cell>
          Jump unconditionally to the instruction at bytecode position 20. (Equivalent to
          JUMP)
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <p><c>99 00 14</c></p>
          <p><c>(10011001 00000000 00010100)</c></p>
        </cell>
        <cell><c>ifeq 20</c></cell>
        <cell>Conditional</cell>
        <cell>
          Pop the top integer from the stack; if it equals zero, jump to bytecode position
          20. (Equivalent to JUMP_IF_ZERO)
        </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    The Java Virtual Machine is a software program that simulates a computer. It has its
    own virtual registers, its own memory management system, and its own instruction set.
  </p>

  <p>
    While the bytecode in your <c>.class</c> file is always identical regardless of where
    it was compiled, the JVM itself is platform-specific. There is a Windows JVM for x86
    processors, a macOS JVM for Apple Silicon processors, a Linux JVM for cloud servers,
    and an Android JVM for mobile devices:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="figure-write-once-run-anywhere">
    <caption>Write once, run anywhere architecture</caption>
    <image source="figures/figure_1_3.png" width="85%">
      <shortdescription>Diagram showing Java bytecode branching to Windows, macOS, and Linux JVMs, which translate instructions for distinct physical CPU architectures.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    When you type <c>java MyProgram</c>, the operating system launches the native JVM. The
    JVM loads your <c>.class</c> bytecode, translates those virtual instructions into the
    host computer's native machine language, and coordinates with the physical CPU to
    execute your program.
  </p>

  <p>
    This two-step architecture fulfilled Java’s famous founding motto: <q>Write Once, Run
    Anywhere</q> (WORA). You can write and compile a Java application on your Windows
    laptop, email the <c>.class</c> file to a friend running macOS, and upload it to a
    Linux web server—the exact same bytecode runs everywhere without modification.
  </p>

  <p>
    In early versions of Java, the JVM was a straightforward interpreter, executing
    bytecode instructions one by one. While this made Java portable, early Java programs
    were noticeably slower than C++.
  </p>

  <p>
    Modern JVMs solve this performance gap using a sophisticated technology called a
    Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        When your program first starts up, the JVM begins executing by interpreting bytecode
        quickly so the program launches without delay.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        While the program runs, the JVM actively monitors its execution to identify <q>hot
        spots</q>—sections of code, such as loops and frequently called methods, where the
        program spends most of its time.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The JIT compiler takes those hot-spot bytecode sections and compiles them directly
        into optimized native machine code in memory on the fly.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The next time that code is called, the CPU runs the ultra-fast native machine code
        directly, completely bypassing bytecode translation!
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Thanks to the JIT compiler, modern Java combines the universal portability of an
    interpreted language with execution speeds that rival fully compiled languages like C
    and C++. As a programmer, you get the best of both worlds: write your code once in Java,
    and let the compiler and JVM deliver fast, consistent performance on any device.
  </p>
</section>